<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eucharist</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lord's Supper, Lord’s Supper, Holy Communion</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Eucharist, also called Holy Communion or the Lord's Supper, is the Christian rite of bread and wine commemorating the Last Supper of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>. Its meaning divided the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>: Catholics taught transubstantiation, Luther a real bodily presence, and Zwingli a symbolic memorial, a disagreement left unresolved at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marburg-colloquy">Marburg Colloquy</a> of 1529. It remains the central act of worship in most Christian churches.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eucharist.jpg" alt="Eucharist" width="320" height="285" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nheyob, cropped by Tahc · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St_Michael_the_Archangel,_Findlay,_OH_-_bread_and_wine_crop_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
